questions.answer:
const pipe = (...fns) => (input) =>
  fns.reduce((acc, fn) => fn(acc), input);

const trim = (s) => s.trim();
const lower = (s) => s.toLowerCase();
const slug = (s) => s.replace(/\s+/g, "-");

const slugify = pipe(trim, lower, slug);
console.log(slugify("  Hello World  "));
// hello-world

pipe applies left to right: trim removes the spaces, lower lowercases, slug replaces whitespace runs with a hyphen. Each stage is unary (one input, one output), which is what makes them composable. compose is the same reduce with reduceRight, so compose(slug, lower, trim) gives the same result. The empty-array case returns the input unchanged, which is a reasonable identity pipeline. If you need to debug, insert a tap stage: const tap = (label) => (value) => (console.log(label, value), value);.

