questions.answer:
const groupBy = (keyFn) => (items) =>
  items.reduce((acc, item) => {
    const key = keyFn(item);
    (acc[key] ??= []).push(item);
    return acc;
  }, {});

const byRole = groupBy((u) => u.role);
console.log(byRole(users));
// {
//   admin: [ { name: 'Kakade', role: 'admin' }, { name: 'Rohan', role: 'admin' } ],
//   editor: [ { name: 'Asha', role: 'editor' } ]
// }

The curried shape lets you create byRole once and reuse it on any array. reduce starts with {} so an empty input returns {}, not undefined. `acc[key] ??= []` initializes the bucket on first use; push mutates only the local accumulator, which is normal inside reduce, not the input. Object keys are strings, so a numeric keyFn would collapse 1 and "1"; use a Map if key identity matters. For untrusted keys, Object.create(null) avoids collisions with __proto__.

