questions.answer:
function curry(fn) {
  return function curried(...args) {
    return args.length >= fn.length
      ? fn(...args)
      : (...next) => curried(...args, ...next);
  };
}

const format = curry((greeting, name, mark) => `${greeting}, ${name}${mark}`);
console.log(format("Hi")("Kakade")("!"));
console.log(format("Hello", "Asha")("?"));
// Hi, Kakade!
// Hello, Asha?

The inner function accumulates arguments. When enough have arrived (args.length >= fn.length), it calls the original; otherwise it returns a function that appends the next batch and recurses. This supports both fully unary calls and grouped calls, which is more flexible than a hard-coded a => b => c chain. Limitations: fn.length stops at the first default or rest parameter, so a function like (a, b = 1, c) has length 1 and curries incorrectly. Arrow functions cannot be constructed with new, and `this` is not forwarded — use fn.apply(this, args) if the curried function is a method.
